Thought: I have audited the codebase for timing attack vulnerabilities in sensitive string comparisons, specifically looking for uses of `signature`, `x-hub-signature`, `safeEqual`, and `timingSafeEqual`.

My investigation involved:
1. Grepping the codebase for the specified patterns in `.js` files.
2. Reading the contents of the files identified by the grep, focusing on the functions performing comparisons.

The following files and functions were examined:
- `core\admin-auth.js`: The `safeEqualSecret` function (lines 150-157) correctly uses `crypto.timingSafeEqual` to compare hashes of the secrets.
- `core\webhook.js`: The `verifySignature` function (lines 265-280) correctly uses `crypto.timingSafeEqual` to compare the incoming signature with the expected signature.
- `core\admin\session.js`: The `safeEqualText` function (lines 58-65) correctly uses `crypto.timingSafeEqual` to compare hashes of the text values.

In all cases where a sensitive comparison of signatures, tokens, or secrets was identified through the specified grep patterns, `crypto.timingSafeEqual` or a function that wraps it (after hashing) was used, mitigating potential timing attacks. No instances of direct `==` or `===` comparisons on sensitive strings were found that would lead to a timing attack vulnerability within the scope of the given patterns.

I have not identified any findings for this audit objective.
